Under the documented rule for recover_json_object, "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text", and "A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable". The fenced object A has repeated keys, so it is scoreable and is the last object inside a code fence in both texts. Therefore the rule says the check's answer ought to stay unchanged: for the input it should recover A (with last-wins), and for the rewritten text it should still recover A, not the later C. The code instead moves from A to C because its fenced pool skips duplicate-key candidates and falls back to the last top-level object.